## 1.2 Context Description

The WordPress CMS is a web-based Content Management System that allows users to create, manage, publish, and view digital content. The system provides an admin dashboard for content authors and administrators, while site visitors access the public website through a web browser.

In the context diagram, WordPress CMS is shown as the central system. It interacts with several external actors and services. Site visitors browse published content, content authors create and edit posts or pages, and site administrators manage users, themes, plugins, and settings. Plugin developers extend the system by adding new functionality.

The WordPress CMS also communicates with external systems such as an email service for notifications, CDN for faster static content delivery, OAuth provider for secure login, cloud storage for media files, analytics tools for usage tracking, search engine services for content indexing, and payment gateways for paid features or subscriptions.

This context view helps to understand the system boundary, external dependencies, and the main users who interact with WordPress.

