# 5. Architecture Design Decisions

Architecture design decisions explain the important choices made by the group while designing the WordPress CMS architecture.

| Decision ID | Architecture Design Decision | Reason / Justification |
|---|---|---|
| ADD-01 | Select WordPress as the CMS platform. | WordPress is widely used, easy to manage, open-source, and suitable for blogs, business websites, and dynamic content websites. |
| ADD-02 | Use modular monolithic architecture. | WordPress core runs as one main application, but functionality is separated through themes, plugins, and core modules. This keeps deployment simple and manageable. |
| ADD-03 | Use plugin-based extensibility. | Plugins allow new features such as SEO, contact forms, security, analytics, and e-commerce without modifying the core WordPress code. |
| ADD-04 | Use theme-based presentation layer. | Themes separate website design from content, allowing users to change the website appearance without affecting stored data. |
| ADD-05 | Use MySQL/MariaDB as the primary database. | WordPress officially supports relational databases like MySQL/MariaDB, which are reliable for storing posts, pages, users, comments, settings, and metadata. |
| ADD-06 | Use role-based access control. | Different users need different permissions. Administrators, authors, editors, and visitors should not have the same access level. |
| ADD-07 | Use CDN and caching for performance. | CDN and cache reduce server load and improve page loading speed for visitors. |
| ADD-08 | Use shared media storage. | In scalable deployment, multiple WordPress instances need access to the same uploaded images, videos, and documents. |
| ADD-09 | Use backup and monitoring services. | Backups and monitoring improve reliability and help recover the website after failure, attack, or data corruption. |
| ADD-10 | Use HTTPS, WAF, and security plugins. | These protect WordPress from common web attacks, insecure traffic, brute force attempts, and plugin vulnerabilities. |

